  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Thursday...\n\nWarm & dry conditions will persist through the weekend and into\nnext week, courtesy of a ridge over the western CONUS that is flat\nenough to keep monsoon moisture well to our south.\n\nMesoanalysis shows 850mb cold advection over north central MT\napproaching our north. The front's arrival plus daytime mixing\nwill yield breezy NW winds this afternoon and early evening. This\nplus warm temps and low RHs (teens) will keep fire wx conditions\nelevated. A Red Flag Warning remains in effect til 8pm over\neastern fire zones. Backdoor cooling arrives tonight and this\nleads to a slightly cooler day Friday, with less wind (though\nmixed W-NW winds remain somewhat breezy along far western \nfoothills tomorrow). Thermal trof deepens over us Saturday,\nfollowed by another wind shift to breezy N winds over a larger\narea. Saturday is also looking quite warm (near 100F) and dry\n(8-12% RH, per pwat min on this day), and thus is the next day to\nexpect elevated fire weather conditions. There remains \nuncertainty with regard to wind speeds and magnitude of low RHs,\nso something to monitor. Sunday will be a bit cooler and more\nhumid, but with temps still in the 90s and RHs in the teens. All\ntold, fire weather conditions remain elevated thru the weekend.\nPlease be careful not to spark a fire!\n\nIt appears that next week we will see a gradual increase in plains\nand perhaps monsoon moisture. Ensembles are in good agreement in\nshowing pwats returning to above normal and possibly near an inch\nby Tuesday. This combined with a series of weak shortwaves coming\noff the Pacific will lead to increasing thunderstorm potential. \nThe main risk of TS looks to initially be in our east Monday & \nTuesday, but by midweek this should extend to western areas as \nwell. There is also potential for a stronger wave to emerge by \nlate week, but uncertainty increases significantly by then. In \naddition to the increasing humidity will be a gradual drop in \ntemperatures. Look for highs in the upper 80s to mid 90s early, \ndropping to the 80s by Thursday.\n\nAdditionally, wildfire smoke will continue to promote hazy skies \nand occasional surges of poorer air quality w/ any frontal \npassages for the foreseeable future. There is a tremendous amount \nof smoke being produced by the fires over the PacNW.\n\nJKL",
